WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.012 --> 00:00:16.701
Hi, I’m Sam Schultz.

00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:21.029
I’m new here on Journey to the Microcosmos
and I’ve been put up to a very questionable

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.801
task for my first episode.

00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:27.099
While the other new hosts get to talk about
beautiful microbes like the Beggiatoa, I am

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:29.821
here to talk to you about butts.

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:31.517
A whole compilation of them.

00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:33.684
And I know why I’ve been 
asked to do this.

00:00:33.684 --> 00:00:37.700
I’m a producer for SciShow and one of the
hosts for the lightly competitive science

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.399
game show podcast SciShow Tangents.

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:44.390
And each of our episodes ends with a butt
fact, where one of our panelists recites a

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.536
fact about butts or poop or something
vaguely butt-related.

00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:52.860
We’ve also been known to argue about important
existential questions that can shake you to

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.453
your very core, like…is butt legs?

00:00:56.453 --> 00:01:00.730
But as someone who is on Team “Butt is Legs,”
I have to admit that microbes make a compelling

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:06.317
argument for the opposition, because so many
of them don’t have legs but still have a butt.

00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:10.739
Like in this first video, about the flowery-looking
animal called Bryozoa.

00:01:10.739 --> 00:01:14.261
When you first see them, you’ll probably
be like, “Wait, where’s the butt?”

00:01:14.261 --> 00:01:17.909
But stick around, and you’ll find out why
that’s actually a very important question

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:20.409
for scientists studying the Bryozoa.

00:01:25.195 --> 00:01:29.540
If I were to ask you to just do a little sketch
of what you think of when you hear the phrase

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:35.409
“moss animals,” you might imagine something
like this tardigrade—a waddling, slightly

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:42.380
fantastical creature that we did in fact find
living within the minuscule greenery of moss.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.787
But depending on who you 
are and what you know,

00:01:45.787 --> 00:01:48.244
maybe you draw something completely different.

00:01:48.244 --> 00:01:52.926
Maybe you draw something more like this.

00:01:52.926 --> 00:01:56.663
These don’t really look much like an animal
at all, do they?

00:01:56.663 --> 00:02:01.659
At first glance, they seem a bit more like
plants, with a stalk connecting a series of

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:04.189
flowers with thin, elegant petals.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:07.147
But no, that is indeed an animal.

00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:12.349
One that has the dubious honor of being defined
largely by its anus.

00:02:16.528 --> 00:02:21.679
Before we get there though, let’s start with just 
explaining what these animals actually are.

00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:27.295
These organisms are members of the phylum
Bryozoa, a name that translates in Greek to

00:02:27.295 --> 00:02:28.930
“moss animals”.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.260
But Bryozoa aren’t actually animals that
live in moss.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.190
They get their name because of what they look
like when all of the elegant individuals you

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:43.481
see here congregate into colonies that look,
ya know, mossy.

00:02:43.481 --> 00:02:49.290
That giant gelatinous mass seems to bear little
resemblance to the tiny winsome creatures

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.398
that make it up.

00:02:50.398 --> 00:02:57.274
But Bryozoa are notable in the animal kingdom
because of just how colonial the phyla is.

00:02:57.274 --> 00:03:02.910
Around the world, in waters salty and not,
are colonies of different shapes and sizes

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.849
made up of individuals like these.

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:08.829
These colonies can get their start in different
ways.

00:03:08.829 --> 00:03:14.380
Some might be the result of sexual reproduction,
sperm and egg that could have met in the waters

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.819
away from their makers.

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:22.340
Or, in other cases, one colony may have captured
sperm released by another, using it to fertilize

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:28.470
its own eggs, which stay brooding until a
larva emerges, ready to set out and develop

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.610
its own colony.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.757
But Bryozoa have another asexual option for
reproduction.

00:03:34.757 --> 00:03:40.060
Instead of fertilizing an egg, a colony can
amass a bunch of cells into a structure like

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.707
this one, called a statoblast.

00:03:43.707 --> 00:03:49.810
These statoblasts can be so prevalent in our
samples that James, our master of microscopes,

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:54.000
has to scoop them out to keep them from being
the main focus of our videos.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.489
They are thick and durable, able to lay dormant
and protect their contents from cold temperatures

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:00.750
and dry weather.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.850
But when the conditions become right again,
they grow into a new individual that will

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.569
form the basis of a new colony.

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:15.709
To do that, the individual Bryozoa, called
a zooid, navigates the waters as a larva,

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:19.563
eventually attaching itself to some kind of
solid surface.

00:04:19.563 --> 00:04:24.470
Perhaps a piece of rock, or some kind of shelf,
maybe even some seaweed.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:29.760
And from there, it will begin to divide, budding
off but keeping its descendants close and

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.320
connected as they increase in number.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:38.750
Now, the arrangement of these zooids dictates
the shape of their colony, as does the material

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.152
they secrete to support the colony overall.

00:04:42.152 --> 00:04:48.614
Some excrete a gelatinous material that turns
them into those masses of jelly we saw earlier.

00:04:48.614 --> 00:04:53.009
Others rely on chitinous materials that give
them more rigidity.

00:04:53.009 --> 00:04:58.190
And as they spread over their surface and
even grow up into the water around them, Bryozoa

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.945
colonies take on many spectacular shapes.

00:05:02.945 --> 00:05:07.282
Even though Bryozoa are different from coral,
they definitely look similar.

00:05:07.282 --> 00:05:10.807
And like coral, Bryozoa colonies don’t move.

00:05:10.807 --> 00:05:14.110
Instead they remain attached to their surface.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:18.230
But while they may not be able to move through
the world around them to gather resources,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:23.590
Bryozoa are able to make the most of their
surroundings, with individual zooids taking

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:29.983
on special functions that allow the group
to accomplish more than the individuals.

00:05:29.983 --> 00:05:36.710
Some focus on making eggs, while others form
small beaks that allow them to snap at predators.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:42.930
And these zooids are all dependent on another
group of zooids that gather food and share

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.310
the nutrients with everyone else.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:51.190
To get that food, the feeding zooids rely
on the lophophore, the ring of tentacles crowning

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:58.067
every zooid’s bodies that looks a bit like
a bunch of thin petals or a very elegant duster.

00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:03.400
Those tentacles are covered in cilia, which
circulate the waters around the Bryozoa, stirring

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.699
currents like the ones you see here so that
it can sweep bits of food like plankton towards

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:10.007
the animal’s mouth.

00:06:10.007 --> 00:06:15.090
And from certain angles, you can see how the
lophophore and the rest of the zooid’s organs

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.699
tuck into a sort of casing, which is a structure
called the cystid.

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:23.940
And while the Bryozoa has various other parts
that we could talk about, what we’re really

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:30.520
here for is the anus, which is a much bigger
deal than you might expect from an animal

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.472
with such a simple digestive system.

00:06:33.472 --> 00:06:39.804
There’s a mouth, an esophagus, and then
a u-shaped gut that leads to the anus.

00:06:39.804 --> 00:06:46.319
Now the issue is really rooted in the existential
crisis at the heart of microcosmos: how are

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:48.784
things related to each other?

00:06:48.784 --> 00:06:51.790
And what does that mean for the names that
we give to things?

00:06:55.021 --> 00:07:01.590
Organisms like these were originally given
the name Polyzoa in 1830, but that was changed

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:06.418
a year later to Bryozoa by the German naturalist
Christian Ehrenberg.

00:07:06.418 --> 00:07:11.050
But as more organisms were added to the group,
the people watching them and describing them

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:16.580
realized that they were actually lumping together
two very similar groups of organisms that

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.370
actually had some key differences between
them.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:24.277
And one of those key differences was where
the organism’s anus was located.

00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:29.810
One group had their anus situated just outside
their lophophore—that tentacle crown we

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.940
were watching gather food earlier.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:36.590
And one had their anus situated just inside
their lophophore.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:41.879
So to differentiate between the two groups,
scientists gave them very appropriate names.

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:47.250
One group was called Ectoprocta, which translates
in Ancient Greek to “outside anus”.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.964
And the other were called Entoprocta, which
means “inside anus.”

00:07:52.964 --> 00:07:59.280
Because you can define anything by its butt,
as long as you turn to ancient languages.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.450
In general, Bryozoa typically refers to the
Ectoprocts, though for many reasons (..I can’t

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:08.630
possibly imagine why…), Bryozoa seems to
be the preferred name.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:14.759
But the history and relationship between the
Ectoprocts and Entoprocts remains an ongoing

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:18.475
mystery that scientists continue to explore.

00:08:19.763 --> 00:08:24.387
They are not the only creature out there
to be wrapped up in a phylogenetic mystery.

00:08:24.387 --> 00:08:29.100
But isn’t it great to know, especially for
such a strange animal like this, that the

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.249
mystery is built on butts?

00:08:39.465 --> 00:08:43.942
Imagine if your name translated to “inside
anus” or “outside anus”?

00:08:43.942 --> 00:08:47.870
I guess it sounds better in Ancient Greek,
but still, scientists really make a lot of

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.529
strange choices.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:55.810
Like the subject of our next video: Antoni
Von Leeuwenhoek, the original master of microscopes.

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:58.814
Leeuwenhoek looked for microbes in all sorts
of interesting places.

00:08:58.814 --> 00:09:03.012
But given the subject of our video today,
I think you can figure out one of the more

00:09:03.012 --> 00:09:07.022
unique sources of microbial 
delights that Leeuwenhoek found.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:18.010
In the year 1675, I discovered very small
living creatures in rain water, which had

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:23.872
stood but few days in a new earthen pot glazed
blue within.

00:09:26.471 --> 00:09:32.779
Those are the famous words that Antony Van
Leeuwenhoek used to describe microbes.

00:09:32.779 --> 00:09:38.310
Though he didn’t use the word “microbe”, he
called them animalcules, and the letter itself

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.720
is not exactly the one he wrote.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.190
Leeuwenhoek’s version was written, of course,
in Dutch and then sent off to Henry Oldenburg,

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:50.569
the secretary of the Royal Society in London
who founded the publication

00:09:50.569 --> 00:09:52.584
Philosophical Transactions.

00:09:52.584 --> 00:09:58.500
Oldenberg translated Leeuwenhoek’s work
into English and published it in 1677.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.532
The rest, as they say, is microbiology.

00:10:07.951 --> 00:10:14.300
In 1932, the scientist Clifford Dobell published
a biography of Leeuwenhoek called “Antony

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.834
van Leeuwenhoek and his “Little animals,”.

00:10:17.834 --> 00:10:23.410
Dobell introduces his subject with some self-consciousness
for taking on the story of someone who, if

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:27.290
you’ve been interested in microscopy, you
have almost certainly heard of.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:31.350
And he wrote:
Dear reader: I know full well that you and

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:37.540
everyone else must have met Mr van Leeuwenhoek
many a time before; but please let me reintroduce

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:41.459
him to you, for he is a man worth knowing
more intimately.

00:10:41.459 --> 00:10:47.010
Though he was born exactly 300 years ago he
is still very much alive, and would be glad

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:53.250
to make your better acquaintance—provided
only that you are a “true lover of learning”

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:55.580
(as of course you are).

00:10:56.649 --> 00:11:02.649
The fascination with Leeunwenhoek’s work
for Dobell and so many of us lies in the scope

00:11:02.649 --> 00:11:11.523
of Leeuwenhoek’s curiosity, and the writing that 
documented his many journeys into the microcosmos.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:25.880
Born in the Netherlands in 1632, Leeuwenhoek
was a draper by trade, meaning that he sold cloth.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:30.950
He took up microscopy as a hobby, learning
to grind lenses and turn them into a tool

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.880
to see into tiny worlds.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:38.500
The simple microscopes he built were better
than many of those of his contemporaries,

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:43.439
producing images that were both more magnified
and more clear.

00:11:43.439 --> 00:11:47.779
Those of course compared to what the average
high school biology class has today, they

00:11:47.779 --> 00:11:51.170
were very bad at both of those things.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:57.170
The creatures Leeuwenhoek described in his
1677 publication were not his first description

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.681
of tiny animals in water though.

00:11:59.681 --> 00:12:06.790
In 1674, he wrote of “green streaks, spirally
wound,” a description that later observers

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:11.670
have taken to refer to the filamentous green
algae spirogyra.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:17.100
He also wrote of little creatures he’d found
that were, as he described them, “a thousand

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:23.406
times smaller than the smallest ones I have
ever yet seen upon the rind of cheese.”

00:12:23.406 --> 00:12:27.254
Those little cheese rind creatures he was
describing were likely mites.

00:12:33.012 --> 00:12:36.980
Reading Leeuwenhoek’s work is kind of like
following an old treasure map.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:44.140
His words lay out pathways and clues that help 
us navigate through a drop of 17th century water.

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:49.660
And if you follow that map, at the end you
find a wealth of information, the knowledge

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:55.110
of microbes and their identities that was
still unknown in his time, but that in the

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:57.969
centuries since has been accumulated.

00:12:59.601 --> 00:13:05.760
For Dobell, Leeuwenhoek’s 20th century scientist
biographer, this treasure hunt was a long

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:10.970
one, borne out of his own enchantment with
these original discoveries and a desire to

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:12.670
better understand them.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:17.680
He learned Dutch, only to find that the 17th
century Dutch of Leeuwenhoek’s letters was

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:23.150
impenetrable to him And with World War I raging
around him, he had to set aside his attempts

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.190
to translate Leeuwenhoek’s work for some
time.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:31.180
But eventually, through what seems to be largely
a lot of determination, Dobell was able to

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.100
work his way through Leeuwenhoek’s letters.

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:40.180
And that labor of love led, naturally, to
translating things like Leeuwenhoek’s descriptions

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.751
of his own poop.

00:13:42.751 --> 00:13:48.589
Per Dobell, Leeuwenhoek wrote the following:
I have usually of a morning a well-formed

00:13:48.589 --> 00:13:54.079
stool; but hitherto I have had sometimes a
looseness of the bowels.

00:13:54.079 --> 00:13:59.210
[…] My excrement being so thin, I was at
divers times constrained to examine it.

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:03.209
But Leeuwenhoek wasn’t documenting his stool
just for the sake of it.

00:14:03.209 --> 00:14:09.019
In that sample, he saw an opportunity to observe
the animals within.

00:14:09.019 --> 00:14:15.149
I have at times seen very prettily moving
animalcules […] Their bodies were somewhat

00:14:15.149 --> 00:14:21.040
longer than broad, and their belly, which
was flattened, provided with several feet,

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:25.490
with which they made such a movement through
the clear medium and the globules that we

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:30.199
might fancy we saw a pissabed running up against
a wall.

00:14:30.199 --> 00:14:34.009
That description sounds promising, but Dobell
had a problem.

00:14:34.009 --> 00:14:39.889
Leeuwenhoek had compared his microbes to a
“pissabed,” and like us, Dobell had no

00:14:39.889 --> 00:14:42.900
idea what a “pissabed” is.

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:46.630
Even the original English translation he’d
read shrugged it off as a Dutch term with

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:49.120
no obvious English equivalent.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:55.029
Dobell had to find a 17th century Dutch-English
dictionary to figure out that a “pissabed”

00:14:55.029 --> 00:14:56.697
is a woodlouse.

00:14:56.697 --> 00:15:02.290
And with that reference point, Dobell had enough
to diagnose Leeuwenhoek—very belatedly,

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:09.457
of course—with Giardia, the product of a
flagellate known as Giardia intestinalis.

00:15:12.917 --> 00:15:18.290
At the time that Leeuwenhoek was identifying
the microbial residents of his stool, he almost

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.804
certainly didn’t know their role in his
poor digestion.

00:15:21.804 --> 00:15:27.610
Nor did he of course have any name for them, or 
any of the other microbes that we now know he saw.

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:32.700
And yet we can identify rotifers from his
characterization of their wheeled heads, and

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:36.569
vorticella from his description of their belled
bodies.

00:15:36.569 --> 00:15:41.730
The irony of our centuries-later fascination
with reconstructing and replicating Leeuwenhoek’s

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:45.590
work is that his contemporaries had a much
harder time of it.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:49.970
Leeuwenhoek didn’t help his cause much,
refusing to provide extensive details of his

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:55.010
methods and even keeping his microscope-building
techniques to himself.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:16:00.541
He did, however, provide eight signed testimonies
from various men, including a minister, to

00:16:00.541 --> 00:16:04.600
verify that he had made these observations.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:07.236
Of course, signed testimony is one thing.

00:16:07.236 --> 00:16:09.951
Reproducibility is another.

00:16:09.951 --> 00:16:14.959
Several of Leeuwenhoek’s contemporaries
tried and failed to replicate his observations

00:16:14.959 --> 00:16:21.930
until finally Robert Hooke—the famed microscopist
and writer-slash-illustrator of Micrographia—was

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:25.860
able to see the tiny animalcules Leeuwenhoek
had seen.

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:31.860
Hooke however was willing to outline his specific
methods and demonstrate them, validating the

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:38.967
observations Leeuwenhoek had made and providing
others with the means to make their own microbial ventures.

00:16:38.967 --> 00:16:44.490
And like Dobell centuries later, Hooke was
reportedly so taken with Leeuwenhoek’s work

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.270
that he learned Dutch to read his letters.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:52.993
The legacy of Leeuwenhoek is in the microbes
he uncovered, but of course it was more than that.

00:16:52.993 --> 00:16:59.190
It was also the curiosity that he passed on,
and the acts of interpretation that they inspired—both

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:04.660
literally with all of these scientists taking
up Dutch to just learn from his letters, and

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:09.809
then scientifically as we decipher what microbes
he must have seen.

00:17:09.809 --> 00:17:14.890
And so it seems fitting to close with how
Leeuwenhoek viewed his own little animalcules.

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:19.552
He said, “Among all the marvels 
that I have discovered in nature,

00:17:19.552 --> 00:17:22.726
these are the most marvelous of all.”

00:17:28.606 --> 00:17:32.949
I know I’m supposed to think it’s gross that 
Leeuwenhoek dug into his own poop for microbes.

00:17:32.949 --> 00:17:37.550
But as weird as it is, I also respect the
dedication to his craft.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:42.070
But now I want to know how many other noted scientists
have documented and studied their poop so

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:46.860
thoroughly that future historians can diagnose
them with an intestine parasite.

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:50.284
Is it just Leeuwenhoek, making him a true
legend?

00:17:50.284 --> 00:17:54.351
Well, I’ve probably spent too much time
already thinking about Leeuwenhoek’s butt.

00:17:54.351 --> 00:17:56.578
Which brings me to my next question.

00:17:56.578 --> 00:17:58.424
What’s the big deal about butts anyway?

00:17:58.424 --> 00:18:00.114
Why do we really need them?

00:18:00.114 --> 00:18:02.428
Luckily there’s an animal that can
teach us that:

00:18:02.428 --> 00:18:06.875
flatworms, a worm so simple that 
it doesn’t have an anus.

00:18:12.244 --> 00:18:15.136
There are no ghosts in the microcosmos.

00:18:15.136 --> 00:18:17.669
There is no Halloween in the microcosmos.

00:18:17.669 --> 00:18:23.960
But if there were, this flatworm would fit
in quite nicely with its spectral appearance.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:28.920
It might be navigating its way through a field
of other organisms, but it looks like it had

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:33.630
three minutes to prepare for a costume party
and decided to go with the classic “old

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:36.786
bedsheet with eyeholes” ghost costume.

00:18:36.786 --> 00:18:44.667
And maybe comparing this flatworm (or even
worms in general) to a ghost is kind of apt.

00:18:44.667 --> 00:18:51.659
Ghosts straddle two worlds, and so do worms,
though in their case, instead of bridging

00:18:51.659 --> 00:18:59.209
the realm of the living and dead, worms manage
to stretch between the micro and the macro.

00:18:59.209 --> 00:19:04.694
This creature you’re watching now would
probably be difficult to see without a microscope.

00:19:04.694 --> 00:19:11.543
But on the other end, there are worms that
are meters too long to observe with a microscope.

00:19:11.543 --> 00:19:18.000
And akin to ghosts, worms might seem like
a sort of reduced form of life.

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:24.619
After all, for a lot of us, worms are the
wiggly tubes that we dug up from playgrounds.

00:19:24.619 --> 00:19:30.630
And of all of the worms, flatworms are perhaps
the most simple of all.

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:37.020
But even digging into what it means to be
the simplest worm makes the whole notion of

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.910
simplicity seem ludicrous.

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:49.835
So let’s start with the broader question:
what are worms, other than wiggly, self-mating tubes?

00:19:49.835 --> 00:19:55.920
While we generally use the word “worm”
to describe many different, unrelated, invertebrate,

00:19:55.920 --> 00:20:01.480
tubular animals that belong to a number of
different phyla, there are three main phyla

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:03.000
that people focus on.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:10.130
The first are our flatworm buddies here, who
are known more formally as Platyhelminthes.

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:15.019
Then there is the phylum Nematoda, known less
formally as roundworms.

00:20:15.019 --> 00:20:19.799
We’ve come across nematodes many times in
our journey through the microcosmos, which

00:20:19.799 --> 00:20:26.409
makes sense given that they are one of the
most abundant animals on this planet.

00:20:26.409 --> 00:20:33.000
And last are the Annelida, phylum of our playground
friend, the earthworm.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:39.250
While earthworms are a bit beyond the scope
of the micro, if you will, we’ve also got

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:42.748
this little Stylaria lacustris.

00:20:42.748 --> 00:20:48.480
At the tip of its head is the proboscis, an
antenna-like structure that the worm uses

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:49.970
for feeding.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:58.680
And as we scan past the head, you can see
that the length of the Stylaria’s body is segmented.

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:02.720
Of the three phyla we’ve mentioned, annelids
are the most complex.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:09.410
They have a closed circulatory system with
tubes that transport nutrients and oxygen

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:15.610
throughout their body, as well as a complete
digestive system that has both a mouth to

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:19.405
take in food and an anus to get rid of waste.

00:21:19.405 --> 00:21:27.120
If we think of worms in general as a gut enclosed
within a body wall, then that gut is a tube

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:34.740
and the body is a tube, and you can imagine
that worms are a tube inside a tube.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:39.919
Now what separates these different groups
is the space that separates one of those tubes

00:21:39.919 --> 00:21:41.656
from the other.

00:21:41.656 --> 00:21:48.289
Complex systems inside annelids are due to
what’s called a coelom, a tissue-lined cavity

00:21:48.289 --> 00:21:54.894
that sits between the tube of the digestive
tract and the tube of the body wall.

00:21:54.894 --> 00:22:00.909
Importantly, this cavity is filled with fluid,
which facilitates the development of organs

00:22:00.909 --> 00:22:05.270
along with the transport of nutrients around
the body.

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:11.600
The development of the first coelom was a
very big deal because coeloms don’t just

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:15.300
allow annelids to have complex systems.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:20.789
They connect the seemingly simple organisms
we’re talking about here to more complex

00:22:20.789 --> 00:22:22.796
animals—like us!

00:22:22.796 --> 00:22:30.000
Because annelids and humans are both what
is known as coelomates, or eucoelomates.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.347
We are animals that have coeloms.

00:22:33.347 --> 00:22:39.560
So, thank you to the ancestor that we share
with earthworms who developed the first coelom,

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:45.049
who allowed more complex organisms like you
to exist.

00:22:45.049 --> 00:22:50.313
Nematodes, on the other hand, are what are
called pseudocoelomates.

00:22:50.313 --> 00:22:56.260
They still have a coelom-like fluid-filled
cavity, but the cavity isn’t lined with

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:59.334
tissue like you find in true coeloms.

00:22:59.334 --> 00:23:04.601
Other microscopic pseudocoelomates include
gastrotrichs and rotifers.

00:23:04.601 --> 00:23:12.190
This pseudocoelom is a slight but significant
difference in nematodes when compared to annelids,

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:16.630
corresponding to a slightly less complex body
plan, though nematodes

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:20.650
still do have a complete digestive system.

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:23.769
The flatworms are the simplest of all.

00:23:23.769 --> 00:23:27.769
Starting with the coelom: flatworms don’t
actually have one.

00:23:27.769 --> 00:23:30.509
They are considered acoelomates.

00:23:30.509 --> 00:23:35.850
That area between the gut and body wall where
annelids and nematodes have a cavity full

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:39.900
of fluid is instead full of tissue.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:45.669
This has a few consequences for the flatworm,
but one of the biggest ones is that this area

00:23:45.669 --> 00:23:51.120
can’t support the development of specialized
systems that you see in other animals.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:58.970
The lack of coelom imposes limits on the flatworm’s
body and lifestyle, reducing both its complexity

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:00.424
and size.

00:24:00.424 --> 00:24:04.577
But that does not mean the flatworm lives
a simple life.

00:24:12.449 --> 00:24:19.560
Of the three worm groups, flatworms are the
most like a tube within another tube, except

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:23.270
that their outer tube is much less cylindrical.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:29.179
The “flat” in “flatworm” is actually
an adaptation: without a circulatory system

00:24:29.179 --> 00:24:36.289
or respiratory system, the worm relies on
diffusion across its outer membrane to supply

00:24:36.289 --> 00:24:38.919
cells with oxygen.

00:24:38.919 --> 00:24:46.260
Being flatter gets the cells closer to the
outside and better positioned for diffusion.

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:51.490
And without the ability to build a complete
digestive system like those found in nematodes

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:58.559
and annelids, flatworms have to adjust to
life without that most under-appreciated body

00:24:58.559 --> 00:25:01.120
part of all: the anus.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:07.289
They take in food through one opening, digest
it in their gut, and then spew the waste back

00:25:07.289 --> 00:25:09.820
out from that same opening.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:17.059
So unlike other organisms who have separate
entrances and exits for their food, the flatworm

00:25:17.059 --> 00:25:21.951
must wait to finish digesting before it can
eat again.

00:25:21.951 --> 00:25:26.710
But this is not the only way flatworms can
get rid of waste.

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:33.820
Their body is lined with a special type of
cell called a flame cell that also gets waste

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:35.117
out of the worm.

00:25:35.117 --> 00:25:41.890
While it may not have the elegance of an interconnected
set of organs, this system is its own form

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:43.287
of complexity.

00:25:43.287 --> 00:25:52.449
Basically, every flame cell is one single-celled
kidney, allowing the worm to just...

00:25:52.449 --> 00:25:54.917
ooze waste from its skin.

00:25:54.917 --> 00:26:00.871
And this is just scratching the surface of the 
biological intricacy flatworms are capable of.

00:26:00.871 --> 00:26:08.250
Like many of their more involved worm counterparts,
flatworms can reproduce both asexually and sexually.

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:15.830
And when reproducing sexually, they can mate
with another flatworm, or even with themselves--both

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:19.317
making and fertilizing their own eggs.

00:26:19.317 --> 00:26:25.190
There’s also those flatworm species that
can regenerate, a trick that our more developed

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:28.710
bodies are, alas, not capable of.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:34.758
Even the many weird ways they move make the
word “wiggle” feel reductive.

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:55.254
Our perception of simplicity across nature
is built on comparisons.

00:26:55.254 --> 00:26:58.969
Compared to us, an earthworm may seem simple.

00:26:58.969 --> 00:27:05.120
Put that earthworm next to a flatworm, and
suddenly the earthworm represents a gigantic

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:06.764
biological advancement.

00:27:06.764 --> 00:27:16.694
But then put that flatworm next to a bacteria,
and suddenly it contains multitudes, it is a universe.

00:27:16.694 --> 00:27:25.331
That flatworm is a marvel, an almost unbelievable
testament to the power of evolution.

00:27:29.679 --> 00:27:33.159
I never really thought about how hard it would
be to eat if I had to poop out my mouth at

00:27:33.159 --> 00:27:37.800
the same time, but yeah, I guess I have a
newfound appreciation for the fact that our

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:41.549
bodies are equipped with an easy exit strategy
for waste.

00:27:41.549 --> 00:27:43.750
But you know who I wish would poop a little
less?

00:27:43.750 --> 00:27:45.770
The subject of our next video.

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:49.390
Because now I can’t help but look around
my house and wonder how much of it is covered

00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:50.380
in their poop.

00:27:56.357 --> 00:28:01.220
One of the most frequent questions we get
asked on Journey to the Microcosmos is how

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:07.120
James, our master of microscopes, stays safe
while gathering and studying samples.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:13.019
After all, as beautiful as the microcosmos
is, there are plenty of parasites and pathogens

00:28:13.019 --> 00:28:18.467
in there that we’d prefer to keep under
the microscope and out of our bodies.

00:28:18.467 --> 00:28:23.610
But while James has simple and effective guidelines
to protect his health—like making sure he

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:28.630
always washes his hands before and after handling
samples, and never venturing into a pond with

00:28:28.630 --> 00:28:33.864
an open wound—there are times when those
measures don’t quite cut it.

00:28:33.864 --> 00:28:39.500
These are the times when no matter how much
you protect yourself outside or inside, the

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.000
microcosmos gets the best of you.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:45.263
These are the times of the dust mite.

00:28:47.117 --> 00:28:50.977
Now we don’t usually start these videos
with a warning, but we’re going to do that today.

00:28:50.977 --> 00:28:55.549
While working on this video, at least 4 members
of the Journey to the Microcosmos team found

00:28:55.549 --> 00:29:00.620
themselves having a bit of a crisis about
how clean their homes were.

00:29:00.620 --> 00:29:04.860
If you’ve found yourself sneezing and wheezing
and running around miserable with a runny

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:10.779
nose because of dust, this arachnid is probably
the culprit—though perhaps not in the way

00:29:10.779 --> 00:29:12.000
you would expect.

00:29:14.188 --> 00:29:19.550
Dust mites are small in the grand scheme of
things, but large in the microcosmos, hitting

00:29:19.550 --> 00:29:22.600
just around 300 micrometers in length.

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:27.409
Their bodies are decked out with four pairs
of legs ending in feet that act like suction

00:29:27.409 --> 00:29:33.789
cups as they wander in their quest for three
things: water, food, and darkness.

00:29:35.151 --> 00:29:38.669
Dust mites love humidity because their lives
depend on it.

00:29:38.669 --> 00:29:44.960
They don’t drink water so much as they osmosis
it into them, relying on a salt-filled gland

00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:49.260
near their mouth to absorb water vapor from
the air around them.

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:54.100
So if their surroundings become more arid,
an adult dust mite will wither away until

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:56.201
it eventually dies.

00:29:56.201 --> 00:30:01.659
This is a challenge for dust mites that live
in places with seasons, where wet and rainy

00:30:01.659 --> 00:30:04.440
falls give way to dry winters.

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:10.140
To survive those shifts, they rely on the
resilience of their younger brethren—the

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:14.370
nymphs—whose forms are able to withstand
the lack of moisture.

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:19.120
The seasons of the dust mite’s life was
one of the important clues that helped scientists

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:23.751
in the 20th century understand their role
in dust allergies.

00:30:23.751 --> 00:30:29.440
Scientists identified dust as the source of
some kind of allergen in the 1920s, but they

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:33.386
were not sure what it was exactly about dust
that did it.

00:30:33.386 --> 00:30:37.659
They just knew that people all over the globe
suffered from dust allergies, and that these

00:30:37.659 --> 00:30:44.405
allergies were often seasonal, peaking in
the autumn, particularly after warm, humid summers.

00:30:44.405 --> 00:30:50.149
And other possible sources, like animal dander
or mold, just didn’t quite fit right with

00:30:50.149 --> 00:30:52.409
the seasonality of the allergy.

00:30:52.409 --> 00:30:59.149
But in the 1960s, scientists in the Netherlands
and Japan realized that the dust mite might

00:30:59.149 --> 00:31:00.928
be the culprit.

00:31:00.928 --> 00:31:06.029
They’re found in large numbers in the dust
that lines the unwashed and untouched corners

00:31:06.029 --> 00:31:11.940
of our homes, their populations peaking in
time with the runny noses and bleary eyes

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:13.960
of dust allergies.

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:18.260
But while experiments confirmed that mites
really were the source of people’s sensitivities

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:22.615
to house dust, it wasn’t clear what made
them so special.

00:31:22.615 --> 00:31:24.351
So, we’ll get to that in a bit...

00:31:24.351 --> 00:31:30.530
James often finds dust mites in the humidity
chambers he uses to keep his microbes alive.

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:35.370
These chambers are made up of a dish lined
with wet toilet paper, which is just about

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:40.360
all a dust mite needs to feel right at home—especially
when there’s potentially some delicious

00:31:40.360 --> 00:31:42.149
microbes to munch on.

00:31:42.149 --> 00:31:47.220
So to keep those other microbes from becoming
a mite meal, James has to regularly clean

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:50.074
out and disinfect the humidity chamber.

00:31:50.074 --> 00:31:54.000
Now, that cleaning might get rid of the dust
mites in the humidity chamber, but the problem

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:01.710
is that our houses are full of food for them
because our houses are full of us—of flakes

00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:07.460
from our skin that shed and gather all around
and sustain the invisible mite.

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:11.899
And this is a problem not just because it
means the mite can thrive in our homes.

00:32:11.899 --> 00:32:16.247
It’s a problem because food means poop.

00:32:16.247 --> 00:32:21.340
Over the course of its life, a mite will produce
about 1000 pieces of poop that are roughly

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:24.000
the size of a grain of pollen.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:30.150
Inside those bits of fecal matter are enzymes
that help the dust mite eat its own poop and

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:34.040
get nutrients that it might not have gotten
the first time around.

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:40.960
But should the dust mite choose to not revisit
its prior meals, the feces will float around

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:45.811
the room, attached to other particles until
eventually they settle down—perhaps on a

00:32:45.811 --> 00:32:48.976
pillow, or on a pet’s bed, or a car seat.

00:32:48.976 --> 00:32:56.032
It’s like we’re living in a gigantic snow
globe, except that the snow... is dust mite feces.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:04.867
In 1981, researchers confirmed the bad news
about this animal’s poop— people are allergic to it.

00:33:04.867 --> 00:33:09.460
To be more specific, they confirmed that dust
mite poop contains specific proteins that

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:12.070
many people are allergic to.

00:33:12.070 --> 00:33:15.610
So if you’re airing out some sheets and
you start sneezing, what you might actually

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:19.753
be reacting to is dust mite feces flying around
the air.

00:33:19.753 --> 00:33:24.820
Now, we apologize for this mental image but
it is reality, and it has embedded in our

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:27.789
heads, so we have to share it with you as
well.

00:33:27.789 --> 00:33:33.169
But we can offer what might be a small comfort:
there is another allergen from dust mites

00:33:33.169 --> 00:33:35.369
that has nothing to do with poop.

00:33:35.369 --> 00:33:41.910
Dust mites have a fairly lengthy mating process,
sometimes taking up to two days to finish—this

00:33:41.910 --> 00:33:45.779
is a pretty lengthy time for any organism,
but especially for an animal that’s only

00:33:45.779 --> 00:33:49.130
got about 100 total days to live.

00:33:49.130 --> 00:33:53.779
Over the course of their life, the female
dust mite will lay up to 80 eggs, which hatch

00:33:53.779 --> 00:33:59.769
into larvae that then go through several different
stages of development before becoming adults

00:33:59.769 --> 00:34:05.250
With each passing stage, the dust mite sheds
its exoskeleton, leaving behind its youth.

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:10.340
And that exoskeleton provides some of the
other allergens for people react to.

00:34:10.340 --> 00:34:14.839
But though that is something people are sometimes
allergic to, it really is mostly just the

00:34:14.839 --> 00:34:17.120
poop that sets off people’s allergies.

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:21.298
So I guess there really wasn’t that much
comfort there after all.

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:30.559
And even for a trained and cautious microbiologist,
dust mites can become an unwelcome surprise.

00:34:30.559 --> 00:34:35.790
Once, James brought home some samples taken
from a water dish that his neighbor left outside

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:37.010
for their cat.

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:42.813
The surface was covered in tiny round things,
and James thought they might be rotifers.

00:34:42.813 --> 00:34:47.159
But when he looked at the surface of the scum
under the microscope, there were no charming

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:48.929
rotifers to be found.

00:34:48.929 --> 00:34:53.350
It was mites, just hundreds of them crawling
around the slide.

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:58.751
It was so unpleasant that James immediately
bleached the slide.

00:34:58.751 --> 00:35:03.170
Now, he’s not sure if that sample is the
reason, but for days after, James kept sneezing

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:08.290
and having to deal with a runny nose, all
of which pointed to a potential mite invasion.

00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:12.986
Fortunately though, our homes are not a utopia
for dust mites.

00:35:12.986 --> 00:35:17.840
What they really need is dark, which is why
they prefer to dig themselves deep into carpets

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:21.036
and other soft things that give them space
to burrow4.

00:35:21.036 --> 00:35:25.196
Dust mites have a harder time with materials
like suede that are difficult to hide themselves

00:35:25.196 --> 00:35:29.790
in, and they usually avoid hard surfaces
that are exposed to light.

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:35.090
So for James, his weapons against the mite
invasion were clear: a vacuum, a mattress

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:38.020
cleaner, and a strong UV light bulb.

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:41.690
It is one of the more ignoble ends to one
of our organisms.

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:45.614
We don’t like to hurt them, unless they
are hurting us.

00:35:47.218 --> 00:35:53.079
But I am sure there’s a dust mite somewhere
in our homes right now, settling into a soft,

00:35:53.079 --> 00:35:58.079
dark abode of its own, with hardly a care
in the world for the battle that was waged

00:35:58.079 --> 00:36:00.030
to bring you this video.

00:36:04.257 --> 00:36:05.340
I don’t know what’s grosser.

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:08.830
The idea of eating and pooping out of the
same hole, or the fact that I’m constantly

00:36:08.830 --> 00:36:10.869
inhaling dust mite poop.

00:36:10.869 --> 00:36:12.793
So let’s end on a happier note,

00:36:12.793 --> 00:36:17.930
a festival of feces, if you will, unfolding
in the diverse strategies used by both single-celled

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:21.748
and multicellular organisms to get rid of
their waste.

00:36:25.028 --> 00:36:29.386
Ah, food, one of life’s great pleasures.

00:36:29.386 --> 00:36:34.640
Whether you’re an organism whose body can
create its own food, or you make do with the

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:41.669
consumption of other organisms, food sustains
and connects life both large and small.

00:36:41.669 --> 00:36:44.014
But we’re not going to talk about food today.

00:36:44.014 --> 00:36:47.470
We’re going to talk about what happens…after.

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:51.904
No great meal comes without consequences.

00:36:51.904 --> 00:36:57.900
Even photosynthetic microbes produce oxygen
they don’t need, a byproduct that is quite

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:01.980
useful to many other organisms, including
us.

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:07.690
And for organisms that have to digest their
food to extract nutrients, well, there’s

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:11.680
all sorts of stuff left behind when the process
is done.

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:18.079
In the world of the microcosmos, there are
not only many varied tiny organisms.

00:37:18.079 --> 00:37:24.000
There are also many varied ways to produce
even tinier poop.

00:37:29.801 --> 00:37:33.080
And when we say “tinier,” we mean it.

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:37.410
If you’re worried about being grossed out
by this video, or you really want to make

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:41.760
sure you’re not missing anything, just know
that some of this pooping will require you

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:46.810
to pay very close attention.

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:52.190
Some of this will probably look similar to
macroscopic bowel movements, and some will

00:37:52.190 --> 00:37:53.860
seem less so.

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:59.470
So let’s start with the more poop-like poop
that comes from some of the multicellular

00:37:59.470 --> 00:38:06.490
members of the microcosmos, particularly the
ones with digestive systems complete with

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:12.524
specialized regions for digesting food and
releasing solid waste.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:21.990
Our dragon-like friend the gastrotrich is
doing the old scrunch-and-poop, probably after

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:26.764
having finished a nice meal made up of bacteria
or protozoa.

00:38:26.764 --> 00:38:32.640
The food came in through its pharynx and was
then sent through the intestine, a straight

00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:35.619
tube full of digestive cells.

00:38:35.619 --> 00:38:42.840
After, the remains continued their way to
the end of the tube, where lies the anus--a

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:48.634
microscopic gateway out of the gastrotrich
and into the world.

00:38:48.634 --> 00:38:54.420
That’s all well and good for the more complex
organisms, but how does one poop when one

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:57.126
does not have a butthole?

00:38:57.126 --> 00:39:02.765
Well, for some single-celled organisms, one
becomes the butthole.

00:39:07.151 --> 00:39:10.140
Such is the case for amoebas.

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:17.800
Their formlessness allows them to easily shift
their shape around to take in food via phagocytosis.

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:23.990
The amoeba here happens to have found a nice
meal made of a ciliate, which is now contained

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:28.230
in a food vacuole filling with digestive enzymes.

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:34.560
Those enzymes will eventually break the ciliate
down, allowing the amoeba to absorb nutrients

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:37.110
into its cytoplasm.

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:40.176
But then what happens with the remains?

00:39:40.176 --> 00:39:44.941
Well, as we see with this amoeba here, it
gets released.

00:39:44.941 --> 00:39:52.359
Amoebas and other similar organisms use a
process called exocytosis to send waste back

00:39:52.359 --> 00:39:56.114
to the membrane where it is removed from their
body.

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:04.320
You can see this again here in this Mastigamoeba,
slowly gliding along the microscope slide

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:08.030
while a round bit seems to come off up top.

00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:15.466
It almost looks like it’s leaving behind
a little piece of itself, which I mean, maybe it is.

00:40:16.631 --> 00:40:23.410
Ciliates have a more refined approach to pooping,
which is to say they have the unicellular

00:40:23.410 --> 00:40:27.751
equivalent of an anus called the cytoproct.

00:40:27.751 --> 00:40:34.740
Ciliates gather food into an oral groove,
and then consume it in the digestive vacuoles

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:36.277
that travel through the cell.

00:40:36.277 --> 00:40:43.420
Eventually, when the vacuole makes its way
to the cytoproct, the contents get out, and

00:40:43.420 --> 00:40:45.711
there you have ciliate poop.

00:40:47.484 --> 00:40:53.910
The way organisms poop—the way microbes
poop—are even more varied and wonderful

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:56.119
than we’ve been able to show you here.

00:40:56.119 --> 00:41:01.510
But you might be wondering how you even get
from something relatively simple like this

00:41:01.510 --> 00:41:07.250
ciliate releasing a small bit of waste to
something more complex, like this tardigrade

00:41:07.250 --> 00:41:11.285
with its more compartmentalized approach to
defecation.

00:41:11.285 --> 00:41:17.160
Well the answer, of course, is evolution,
but it’s an answer that leads to many more

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:19.050
unanswered questions.

00:41:19.050 --> 00:41:24.729
Even within animals, the evolution of the
anus—or in some species, the cloaca—is

00:41:24.729 --> 00:41:32.180
not well understood, as discussed in a 2015
review paper with the spectacular title, “Getting

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:35.853
to the bottom of anal evolution”.

00:41:35.853 --> 00:41:42.760
While the anus itself is linked to the evolution
of a digestive system, not all metazoans have

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:44.760
a designated anus.

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:51.010
The hydra gut has only one opening, which
means that the organism’s mouth has to pull

00:41:51.010 --> 00:41:55.740
digestive double duty and open back up to
release the waste.

00:41:55.740 --> 00:42:01.960
And how that system fits in with the evolution
of digestion overall is unclear.

00:42:01.960 --> 00:42:09.550
Much like the evolution of sex, anuses are
a trait that appears and disappears in evolutionary

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:17.210
lineages, reflecting the fact that they might
be useful for some animals and less so for others.

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:22.583
The duality of the hydra’s mouth seems to
be working out great for them after all.

00:42:22.583 --> 00:42:29.418
But having a digestive system with a distinct
entrance and exit is pretty handy too.

00:42:29.418 --> 00:42:31.806
For one, it’s just a little less disgusting.

00:42:31.806 --> 00:42:35.930
But it also keeps food flowing in one direction.

00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:42.359
And that means animals like the tardigrade
and us don’t have to wait to finish digesting

00:42:42.359 --> 00:42:44.263
to eat more food.

00:42:44.263 --> 00:42:45.800
That would be annoying.

00:42:45.800 --> 00:42:53.730
We just get to keep eating, all thanks to
that evolutionary mystery that is the anus.

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:57.444
In the end though, anus or not, everybody
poops.

00:42:57.444 --> 00:43:02.710
Food, after all, might be one of life’s
great pleasures, but all good things must

00:43:02.710 --> 00:43:04.800
come to an end.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:10.770
And perhaps, with the microcosmos, we can
find some pleasure in that as well.

00:43:13.710 --> 00:43:18.609
And that is the conclusion of our journey through
the hind end of the microcosmos.

00:43:18.609 --> 00:43:20.670
It was beautiful, it was gross.

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:25.580
And I hope, most of all, that unlike the nutrients
these organisms were getting rid of, this

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:27.886
video was not a waste.

00:43:27.886 --> 00:43:34.119
So, thank you for coming on this journey with us 
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:43:34.119 --> 00:43:38.086
Before we go, we’d also like to say thank
you to each and every one of our Patrons.

00:43:38.086 --> 00:43:41.599
Some of their names are on the screen right
now, and these are the people that make this

00:43:41.599 --> 00:43:45.700
channel, and videos like this, possible and
for that we are so grateful.

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:51.230
If you’d like to become one of them, you
can go to patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:43:51.230 --> 00:43:54.550
And if you’d like to see more from our Master
of Microscopes, James Weiss, you can check

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:59.440
out Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram, and if you’d
like to see more from us, there’s probably

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:01.631
a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

